There are \(9\) balls total: \(5\) red and \(4\) white.

The probability of getting **at least one white ball** is easiest found using the complement:

\[
P(\text{at least one white}) = 1 - P(\text{no white})
\]

The probability of drawing **no white balls** means drawing \(2\) red balls:

\[
P(\text{no white}) = \frac{\binom{5}{2}}{\binom{9}{2}}
= \frac{10}{36}
= \frac{5}{18}
\]

So,

\[
P(\text{at least one white}) = 1 - \frac{5}{18}
= \frac{13}{18}
\]

\[
\boxed{\frac{13}{18}}
\]

Assuming the two balls are drawn **without replacement**, the probability is \(\boxed{\frac{13}{18}}\).

